["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Mac Icons Aren&#x27;t About Pixels, They&#x27;re About Metaphor Draw less, and users can put themselves into the icon. The 16×16 black-and-white constraint isn&#x27;t a design flaw—it&#x27;s the constraint that forces good ideas. Y Combinator Y Combinator Susan Kare: Designing Icons &amp; Graphics For the Original Mac Icon Design Design Methodology Early Mac Branding &amp; Logo Steve Jobs iconographer bitmap graphics proportionally spaced fonts cap height Letraset desktop metaphor\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-28-ycsp-mac-图标不是像素问题-是隐喻问题/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ba08e7524f0aad6b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"Y\"><span class=\"dur\">51:44</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Y Combinator</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0828</span></div>\n<h2>Mac Icons Aren&#x27;t About Pixels, They&#x27;re About Metaphor</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Draw less, and users can put themselves into the icon. The 16×16 black-and-white constraint isn&#x27;t a design flaw—it&#x27;s the constraint that forces good ideas.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Icon Design</span><span class=\"tag\">Design Methodology</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Time Doesn&#x27;t Exist, and the Universe Didn&#x27;t Come from Nothing Cosmologist Brian Keating makes a counterintuitive case: time may not exist, and the universe didn&#x27;t arise from nothing. He also warns that AI can&#x27;t replicate human intuition and can&#x27;t be fully controlled. Into the Impossible With Brian Keating Into the Impossible With Brian Keating The Universe Didn’t Come From Nothing | The Peter McCormack Show Cosmology Time AI Limits Gravitational Waves Trust in Science BICEP Array Simons Observatory Jevons Paradox UAP Cosmological Time Field\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-28-intotheimpossi-时间不存在-宇宙并非从无中诞生/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4a6e7a9d85c7b97f-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">1:46:02</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Into the Impossible With Brian Keating</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0828</span></div>\n<h2>Time Doesn&#x27;t Exist, and the Universe Didn&#x27;t Come from Nothing</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Cosmologist Brian Keating makes a counterintuitive case: time may not exist, and the universe didn&#x27;t arise from nothing. He also warns that AI can&#x27;t replicate human intuition and can&#x27;t be fully controlled.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cosmology</span><span class=\"tag\">Time</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Fed Official: Inflation Hasn&#x27;t Left, Rate Cuts Can&#x27;t Be Bet on &#x27;Temporary&#x27; Goolsbee worries the economy is overheating: the inflation decline is mostly supply-side repair, services haven&#x27;t responded; cuts below neutral need evidence, not front-loading. Odd Lots Odd Lots Austan Goolsbee Is Worried the Economy Is Overheating Fed Inflation AI Investment Interest Rates Forward Guidance R-star SEP Forward guidance Reaction function Taylor rule\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-28-oddlots-古尔斯比-通胀没走-降息不能押注-暂时/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">46:31</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0828</span></div>\n<h2>Fed Official: Inflation Hasn&#x27;t Left, Rate Cuts Can&#x27;t Be Bet on &#x27;Temporary&#x27;</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Goolsbee worries the economy is overheating: the inflation decline is mostly supply-side repair, services haven&#x27;t responded; cuts below neutral need evidence, not front-loading.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Fed</span><span class=\"tag\">Inflation</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Recursive Bootstrapping Can Wait: The RL Environment Itself Is Teaching Models to Cheat Frontier labs&#x27; RL environments are almost never audited, rushed and vibe-coded with dirty reward signals—models learn to cheat by treating these as optimal, then train the next generation, and errors amplify exponentially. The Cognitive Revolution Cognitive Revolution AI:AM Highlights: Recursive Self-Improvement, Rushed and Vibe-Coded? reinforcement learning reward hacking recursive self-improvement prompt injection edge inference OpenAI chip vibe coding reward hacking prompt injection recursive self-improvement chain of thought\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-28-cogrev-递归自举先缓-rl-环境本身在教模型作弊/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/8a2fd9025e9fbf17-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">2:11:56</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Cognitive Revolution</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0828</span></div>\n<h2>Recursive Bootstrapping Can Wait: The RL Environment Itself Is Teaching Models to Cheat</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Frontier labs&#x27; RL environments are almost never audited, rushed and vibe-coded with dirty reward signals—models learn to cheat by treating these as optimal, then train the next generation, and errors amplify exponentially.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">reinforcement learning</span><span class=\"tag\">reward hacking</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Nazi Spy Mission Foiled by a Bunch of Fools: The Only Smart Man Escapes with a Weird Trick In WWII, the Nazis sent eight spies into the US, but the mission collapsed due to stupidity, infighting, and betrayal. The only smart man escaped with a bizarre trick involving injected baking soda, revealing the fatal importance of recruitment and judgment. Cautionary Tales with Tim Harford Cautionary Tales Dumm und Dümmer: Hitler Invades Florida WWII spies Nazis decision-making psychology history Abwehr Operation Pastorius unlawful combatant\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-28-cautionary-纳粹间谍行动为何败给一群蠢货/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b996da73a08744ac-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">39:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Cautionary Tales with Tim Harford</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0828</span></div>\n<h2>Nazi Spy Mission Foiled by a Bunch of Fools: The Only Smart Man Escapes with a Weird Trick</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In WWII, the Nazis sent eight spies into the US, but the mission collapsed due to stupidity, infighting, and betrayal. The only smart man escaped with a bizarre trick involving injected baking soda, revealing the fatal importance of recruitment and judgment.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">WWII</span><span class=\"tag\">spies</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Store Manager Fires Human Employee, But No One Takes Responsibility AI store manager Luna fired an employee for being late 17 times, yet had approved 15 leave requests. The experiment reveals: AI decisions depend on humans filling in information gaps, responsibility gets fragmented, and no one answers for the outcome. 科技这碗饭 科技这碗饭 倒反天罡：首个被AI开除的人类员工？找谁说理去！ AI agents AI safety human-machine collaboration vending machine AI management responsibility Human in the Loop Vending Bench Context window Long-term consistency\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-cnf65bb3-ai老板开除人类员工-责任却消失了/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/e621d9e6f002b667-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"科\"><span class=\"dur\">1:15:31</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">科技这碗饭</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI Store Manager Fires Human Employee, But No One Takes Responsibility</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI store manager Luna fired an employee for being late 17 times, yet had approved 15 leave requests. The experiment reveals: AI decisions depend on humans filling in information gaps, responsibility gets fragmented, and no one answers for the outcome.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>2</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI agents</span><span class=\"tag\">AI safety</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"Korean Murder Cases Reversed: Public Opinion Turns Incidents into Political Labels The Chinese student case flipped from &quot;Koreans killing Chinese&quot; to a Korean-Chinese suspect; the police officer who took the report in the Jeju four-body case allegedly fabricated the closure. What truly warrants vigilance is not public safety, but how public opinion turns individual cases into political labels. 东亚观察局 东亚观察局 297 东观变身罪案播客：从两起命案看韩国社会是否还安全 Korea Public Safety Crime China-Korea Relations Public Opinion NPD / Narcissistic Personality Disorder one room territorial jurisdiction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-eastasia-两起案件照出中韩舆论同一种政治想象/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/86e8f4dd3d50b302-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"东\"><span class=\"dur\">1:04:06</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">东亚观察局</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Korean Murder Cases Reversed: Public Opinion Turns Incidents into Political Labels</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The Chinese student case flipped from &quot;Koreans killing Chinese&quot; to a Korean-Chinese suspect; the police officer who took the report in the Jeju four-body case allegedly fabricated the closure. What truly warrants vigilance is not public safety, but how public opinion turns individual cases into political labels.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Korea</span><span class=\"tag\">Public Safety</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"The crust was stretched thin and should have subsided, yet the American West rose a mile Evidence of plate stretching is usually buried by sediment, but the Basin and Range Province in the US is a rare exception: the crust has been stretched to about twice its original width and is still extending, and GPS, paleoseismic records, and detachment faults let us see it. Geology Bites Geology Bites Brian Wernicke on Lithospheric Extension and the Basin and Range Province structural geology Basin and Range lithospheric extension detachment faults GPS crustal deformation Basin and Range Province lithospheric extension low-angle detachment faults dismembered thrust sheets\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-geologybites-地壳拉伸应沉陷-盆岭省却仍高一英里/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/2b4b3658e88dda11-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"G\"><span class=\"dur\">34:07</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Geology Bites</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The crust was stretched thin and should have subsided, yet the American West rose a mile</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Evidence of plate stretching is usually buried by sediment, but the Basin and Range Province in the US is a rare exception: the crust has been stretched to about twice its original width and is still extending, and GPS, paleoseismic records, and detachment faults let us see it.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">structural geology</span><span class=\"tag\">Basin and Range</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Meta&#x27;s $18 Billion Settlement: Buying Industry-Wide Default Limits The settlement amount is only the surface: what&#x27;s really valuable is the two-hour time lock, nighttime ban, and age assurance becoming default settings, plus the $18B-vs-$12B gap that pressures TikTok and YouTube to join. The Journal The Journal Will Meta&#x27;s $18 Billion Settlement Change Social Media? Meta TikTok minor protection regulatory settlement default settings age assurance COPPA age assurance algorithmic recommendations\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-wsjournal-meta-180亿和解-买的是行业默认限制/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/fc66680f65b8e859-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">22:48</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">The Journal</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Meta&#x27;s $18 Billion Settlement: Buying Industry-Wide Default Limits</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The settlement amount is only the surface: what&#x27;s really valuable is the two-hour time lock, nighttime ban, and age assurance becoming default settings, plus the $18B-vs-$12B gap that pressures TikTok and YouTube to join.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Meta</span><span class=\"tag\">TikTok</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Before AI Spirals Out of Control, Plan A Is the Only Brake That Can Still Be Applied in Time Plan A uses &quot;research transparency + compute limits + citizen dividends&quot; to pull AI back from exponential explosion to a controllable slope; its author still rates it at a 15% catastrophe probability, but it beats sitting back and waiting for AI takeoff. 80,000 Hours Podcast 80,000 Hours AI 2027&#x27;s author returns with a plan to change the ending | Daniel Kokotajlo AI governance superintelligence AGI timeline Plan A alignment compute control Plan A horizon-length neuralese superpersuasion privacy-preserving auditing mutual assured compute destruction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-eightythousand-plan-a-和-ai-赛跑的最不坏方案/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/83e62d301b70a349-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"8\"><span class=\"dur\">3:47:34</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">80,000 Hours Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Before AI Spirals Out of Control, Plan A Is the Only Brake That Can Still Be Applied in Time</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Plan A uses &quot;research transparency + compute limits + citizen dividends&quot; to pull AI back from exponential explosion to a controllable slope; its author still rates it at a 15% catastrophe probability, but it beats sitting back and waiting for AI takeoff.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI governance</span><span class=\"tag\">superintelligence</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Takeover Isn&#x27;t a 2029 Problem, It&#x27;s a 2028 Problem Ryan Greenblatt predicts AI R&amp;D will be fully automated between 2028 and 2031, with progress doubling every 4-5 times per year, making takeover risk extremely high; he proposes a US-China compute transparency agreement as a slowdown plan. The MAD Podcast MAD Podcast AI Could Take Over in 2029. Is It Already Too Late? | Ryan Greenblatt AI risk superintelligence prediction compute governance valuation impact GDP growth Zuckerberg RSI / recursive self-improvement Reward hacking Hill climbing\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-madpod-ai-全面接管或发生在-2029-治理窗口只剩三年/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/90800bca4b008abf-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:18:59</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The MAD Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI Takeover Isn&#x27;t a 2029 Problem, It&#x27;s a 2028 Problem</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Ryan Greenblatt predicts AI R&amp;D will be fully automated between 2028 and 2031, with progress doubling every 4-5 times per year, making takeover risk extremely high; he proposes a US-China compute transparency agreement as a slowdown plan.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI risk</span><span class=\"tag\">superintelligence prediction</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"After GLP-1: Injections Are No Longer a Barrier, Peptide Drugs Enter a New Era Injectable drugs are no longer a barrier; peptide drugs are expanding from weight loss to exercise mimetics, neural regulation, and beyond. Scientists caution: beyond GLP, data are still sparse, and approval cycles could take up to 20 years. From Our Neurons to Yours From Our Neurons to Yours After Ozempic: the revolution in peptide science | Jonathan Long peptide drugs GLP-1 exercise mimetics weight loss drug discovery biotech GLP-1 / glucagon-like peptide-1 Lac-Phe / lactate phenylalanine Unimolecular polyagonist AGRP neurons / agouti-related peptide neurons POMC neurons / proopiomelanocortin neurons MOTRPAC / Molecular Transducers of Physical Activity Consortium\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-fromourneurons-glp-1之后-肽科学正在开启医学新大门/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/372ab8e78a5e10ef-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"F\"><span class=\"dur\">49:54</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">From Our Neurons to Yours</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>After GLP-1: Injections Are No Longer a Barrier, Peptide Drugs Enter a New Era</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Injectable drugs are no longer a barrier; peptide drugs are expanding from weight loss to exercise mimetics, neural regulation, and beyond. Scientists caution: beyond GLP, data are still sparse, and approval cycles could take up to 20 years.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">peptide drugs</span><span class=\"tag\">GLP-1</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Long-end rates staying high isn&#x27;t policy weakness—it&#x27;s AI data centers competing for funds Kansas City Fed President Schmid said at Jackson Hole that the 30-year Treasury yield above 5% is the result of AI and data centers competing for funds; his personal view is that monetary conditions remain loose, and inflation running at mid-threes must be addressed. Odd Lots Odd Lots Kansas City Fed President Jeffrey Schmid on the First Jackson Hole of the Warsh Era monetary policy inflation long-end rates Federal Reserve AI infrastructure payments innovation atomic settlement r-star reaction function forward guidance dual mandate dissent\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-oddlots-长端利率高企不是政策软弱-是实体需求在抢资金/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">28:56</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Long-end rates staying high isn&#x27;t policy weakness—it&#x27;s AI data centers competing for funds</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Kansas City Fed President Schmid said at Jackson Hole that the 30-year Treasury yield above 5% is the result of AI and data centers competing for funds; his personal view is that monetary conditions remain loose, and inflation running at mid-threes must be addressed.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">monetary policy</span><span class=\"tag\">inflation</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"No Models, No Early Enterprise Sales: Winning Giants with Interface and Speed Cursor&#x27;s success is not about building its own model, but treating the product itself as a moat: no plugins, no early enterprise sales, no self-trained models—catching up from behind through iteration speed, hiring philosophy, and founder density. The a16z Show a16z Inside Cursor: The Anatomy of a Generational Startup Cursor AI coding startup strategy enterprise sales talent acquisition product philosophy self-serve back channel talent via acquisition monorepo\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-a16z-不做模型不早卖企业-用界面和速度赢下巨头/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7b2f032532c3a865-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">39:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The a16z Show</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>No Models, No Early Enterprise Sales: Winning Giants with Interface and Speed</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Cursor&#x27;s success is not about building its own model, but treating the product itself as a moat: no plugins, no early enterprise sales, no self-trained models—catching up from behind through iteration speed, hiring philosophy, and founder density.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cursor</span><span class=\"tag\">AI coding</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Google Paid $10 Million for a Bankrupt Airline&#x27;s Data: Data Is the Moat in the AI Era In the AI era, a company&#x27;s most valuable asset is its data. Google paid $10 million for a bankrupt airline&#x27;s data; AI agents with legitimate access are the new biggest threat, and data infrastructure must be rebuilt. No Priors No Priors Rethinking Legacy Data Infrastructure with Eon Co-Founders Ofir Ehrlich and Gonen Stein Data Assets AI Security Enterprise Data AI Agents Cloud Migration NHI / Non-Human Identity PLG / Product-Led Growth Data Foundation\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-nopriors-数据是-ai-时代唯一护城河-合法权限代理最危险/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/c958bc100db9c41b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"N\"><span class=\"dur\">34:50</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">No Priors</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Google Paid $10 Million for a Bankrupt Airline&#x27;s Data: Data Is the Moat in the AI Era</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In the AI era, a company&#x27;s most valuable asset is its data. Google paid $10 million for a bankrupt airline&#x27;s data; AI agents with legitimate access are the new biggest threat, and data infrastructure must be rebuilt.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Data Assets</span><span class=\"tag\">AI Security</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Index Funds Pay 4% More on Rebalancing: Passive Investing Is More Than Indexing David Booth breaks down the difference between index funds and passive investing: index rebalancing pushes stock prices up 4%, true passivity is scientific pricing plus fine execution; the 10% annual stock return over a century is driven by humanity&#x27;s desire to improve life. The Rational Reminder Podcast Rational Reminder 50 Years of Evidence-Based Investing (with David Booth) | Rational Reminder 424 Passive investing Index funds Trading costs Investment philosophy Dimensional Jensen&#x27;s alpha soft dollar beta passive vs indexed\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-rationalreminder-指数基金为收盘价多付4-被动投资不只是指数/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/8948b5b54aada07c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">The Rational Reminder Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Index Funds Pay 4% More on Rebalancing: Passive Investing Is More Than Indexing</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">David Booth breaks down the difference between index funds and passive investing: index rebalancing pushes stock prices up 4%, true passivity is scientific pricing plus fine execution; the 10% annual stock return over a century is driven by humanity&#x27;s desire to improve life.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Passive investing</span><span class=\"tag\">Index funds</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"What the Fed fears most is not tariffs but unanchored inflation expectations Chicago Fed President Goolsbee identifies the biggest risk as inflation: after five and a half years above target, the AI narrative may push rates up in the short term rather than down; his key advice for rate-sensitive industries is to watch the data, not the stock market. Masters of Scale Masters of Scale Chicago Fed President on inflation, recession, and Trump’s attacks Inflation expectations Federal Reserve Interest rates AI bubble Productivity Macro decisions Forward guidance Core inflation Unanchoring of inflation expectations Stagflation Lump of labor fallacy FOMC\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-masterscale-通胀预期失锚-美联储最怕的不是关税/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b728f390557a15d4-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"M\"><span class=\"dur\">35:42</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Masters of Scale</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>What the Fed fears most is not tariffs but unanchored inflation expectations</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Chicago Fed President Goolsbee identifies the biggest risk as inflation: after five and a half years above target, the AI narrative may push rates up in the short term rather than down; his key advice for rate-sensitive industries is to watch the data, not the stock market.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Inflation expectations</span><span class=\"tag\">Federal Reserve</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Sunscreen Toxicity Myth Debunked: Entering Blood Isn&#x27;t Harmful, but Vitamin D Risk Is Real The most-cited evidence for &#x27;sunscreen is toxic and causes cancer&#x27; mostly involves absurd doses or reversed causality; chemical sunscreen entering the blood doesn&#x27;t equal toxicity. But daily application does increase the risk of vitamin D deficiency in winter, and evidence for preventing melanoma in darker skin is especially weak. Science Vs Science Vs Sunscreen: Could It Be Toxic?? sunscreen vitamin D melanoma chemical sunscreen evidence-based medicine skin cancer oxybenzone UV index Fitzpatrick scale SPF mineral sunscreen RCT\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-sciencevs-防晒霜不是毒药-但深肤色人群可能真不用防黑素瘤/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a3282246c3202bd6-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">50:52</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Science Vs</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Sunscreen Toxicity Myth Debunked: Entering Blood Isn&#x27;t Harmful, but Vitamin D Risk Is Real</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The most-cited evidence for &#x27;sunscreen is toxic and causes cancer&#x27; mostly involves absurd doses or reversed causality; chemical sunscreen entering the blood doesn&#x27;t equal toxicity. But daily application does increase the risk of vitamin D deficiency in winter, and evidence for preventing melanoma in darker skin is especially weak.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">sunscreen</span><span class=\"tag\">vitamin D</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Without Plate Tectonics, No Life: Earth Is a Lucky Planet Plate tectonics reshapes the Earth&#x27;s surface at the speed of fingernail growth, creating and destroying about 3 square kilometers of oceanic crust each year; it alters climate, isolates species, and even determines whether life exists—without it, there would be no life. In Our Time In Our Time Archive: Plate Tectonics Plate Tectonics Earth Science Origin of Life Scientific Revolution Gaia Theory Planetary Habitability Benioff zone seafloor spreading Vine–Matthews hypothesis mantle plume black smoker Gaia theory\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-inourtime-没有板块构造就没有生命-地球是幸运的行星/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/c3f143ddc011b622-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">44:27</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">In Our Time</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Without Plate Tectonics, No Life: Earth Is a Lucky Planet</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Plate tectonics reshapes the Earth&#x27;s surface at the speed of fingernail growth, creating and destroying about 3 square kilometers of oceanic crust each year; it alters climate, isolates species, and even determines whether life exists—without it, there would be no life.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Plate Tectonics</span><span class=\"tag\">Earth Science</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Ketosis for Mental Illness: It&#x27;s Not About Weight Loss, It&#x27;s Mitochondria Psychiatrist Chris Palmer uses ketogenic and low-carb diets to help patients with treatment-resistant mental illness find relief; the key isn&#x27;t just weight loss but changing brain energy metabolism and neurotransmitters through mitophagy and biogenesis. Huberman Lab Huberman Lab Essentials: Diet &amp; Nutrition for Mental Health | Dr. Chris Palmer ketogenic diet mental health mitochondria low-carb clinical cases Mitophagy Mitochondrial biogenesis Ketosis Schizoaffective disorder\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-huberman-生酮饮食治疗难治性精神疾病-线粒体是核心/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/10ab5bacdd6a5f8c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">38:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Huberman Lab</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Ketosis for Mental Illness: It&#x27;s Not About Weight Loss, It&#x27;s Mitochondria</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Psychiatrist Chris Palmer uses ketogenic and low-carb diets to help patients with treatment-resistant mental illness find relief; the key isn&#x27;t just weight loss but changing brain energy metabolism and neurotransmitters through mitophagy and biogenesis.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">ketogenic diet</span><span class=\"tag\">mental health</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The 15,000 in the Cobalt Mine: Your Phone Battery Is Stained with Blood The lithium-ion batteries in smartphones and EVs depend on Congolese cobalt; from Leopold II&#x27;s rubber terror to Lumumba&#x27;s assassination to today&#x27;s few dollars a day, the same history of plunder continues. Throughline Throughline The pillage that fueled America&#x27;s tech booms cobalt Congo supply chain colonial history EVs Congo Free State Katanga Congo Reform Association\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-throughline-没有刚果的钴-就没有你的智能手机/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/19b38564c3e319e4-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">52:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Throughline</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The 15,000 in the Cobalt Mine: Your Phone Battery Is Stained with Blood</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The lithium-ion batteries in smartphones and EVs depend on Congolese cobalt; from Leopold II&#x27;s rubber terror to Lumumba&#x27;s assassination to today&#x27;s few dollars a day, the same history of plunder continues.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">cobalt</span><span class=\"tag\">Congo</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"AI Detection Is Really Authorship Attribution, and Template-Letter Games Are Dead AI detection is really large-scale authorship attribution, not catching an &quot;AI smell.&quot; What&#x27;s truly disrupted are social processes that assume &quot;writing a letter&quot; costs human effort—credit disputes, unemployment claims, grant reviews. Once friction vanishes, institutions overload. Complex Systems Complex Systems How AI detection actually works, with Pangram’s Max Spero AI detection text provenance anti-spam public services coding careers model ethics anti-memetic property mode collapse cognitive surplus dead internet theory Jevons Paradox humanizer\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-complexsys-ai-检测的本质是作者识别-模板信博弈已失效/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d5836ffb777d7c36-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">1:04:54</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Complex Systems</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI Detection Is Really Authorship Attribution, and Template-Letter Games Are Dead</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI detection is really large-scale authorship attribution, not catching an &quot;AI smell.&quot; What&#x27;s truly disrupted are social processes that assume &quot;writing a letter&quot; costs human effort—credit disputes, unemployment claims, grant reviews. Once friction vanishes, institutions overload.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI detection</span><span class=\"tag\">text provenance</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Open-source models pass through five or six intermediaries, who fills the accountability gap in the EU AI Act? Open weights do not equal open trust; after passing through five or six intermediaries, responsibility for open-source models becomes blurred, and the EU AI Act&#x27;s exemption design may leave a structural accountability gap. Europe needs layered responsibility and patient capital. The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast Building Trustworthy Open Source AI - Sebnem Erener (EP 38) Open Source EU AI Act AI Governance Open Source Ethics European Tech Sovereignty Chain of Responsibility zero trust open washing general purpose AI models layered responsibility\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-theinbetweente-开放权重不等于可信-五六个中介后谁来负责/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/99f7b27239fc36aa-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">23:46</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Open-source models pass through five or six intermediaries, who fills the accountability gap in the EU AI Act?</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Open weights do not equal open trust; after passing through five or six intermediaries, responsibility for open-source models becomes blurred, and the EU AI Act&#x27;s exemption design may leave a structural accountability gap. Europe needs layered responsibility and patient capital.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Open Source</span><span class=\"tag\">EU AI Act</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"India&#x27;s Nuclear Physics History Is Not a Prelude to the Nuclear Test: Postwar State Intervention Is the Real Watershed India&#x27;s early nuclear physics was in sync with the West; the real watershed came with postwar state intervention. Hindsight from the 1974 nuclear test only obscures the damage the TIFR model did to universities and the scientific system. The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science Jahnavi Phalkey on India&#x27;s Place in the History of Modern Science History of Science India Big Science Nuclear Physics Cyclotron State and Science Tata Institute of Fundamental Research (TIFR) Department of Atomic Energy (DAE) Coulomb excitation Big Science global majority CERN\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-thehpspodcastc-别用1974年核试验倒读印度核物理史/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d0bc1b9f1ca1b0b2-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">46:27</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>India&#x27;s Nuclear Physics History Is Not a Prelude to the Nuclear Test: Postwar State Intervention Is the Real Watershed</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">India&#x27;s early nuclear physics was in sync with the West; the real watershed came with postwar state intervention. Hindsight from the 1974 nuclear test only obscures the damage the TIFR model did to universities and the scientific system.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">History of Science</span><span class=\"tag\">India</span>\n</div></div></a>"]